.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_WAIT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_wait \- wait for the engine thread to exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_wait(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks the calling thread until the shared reactor of chttpserver
stops completely.  This is true whether something started that stop
before this call, starts it at the same time as this call, or starts
it some time after this call.  This function blocks forever when
nothing ever stops the reactor.  For example, an external SIGTERM
handler may call chttpsvr_engine_stop() at a later, unknown time.

A drop of the last reference to this reactor starts an asynchronous
teardown.  You drop that reference when you destroy the last chttpsvr
with chttpsvr_destroy.  That teardown does not block the caller that
started it.  This function is the explicit escape hatch for a program
that must synchronize with that shutdown, or with a
chttpsvr_engine_stop() call that comes from an external signal.  This
reactor is separate from the engine of chttpclient.  This function
therefore has no connection to the async work of chttpclient in the
same process.

You can call it at the same time from more than one thread.  Each one
blocks until the library confirms that the reactor stopped
completely.  This does not depend on which caller started the stop,
either with a release or with a chttpsvr_engine_stop() call.

Do not call this function from a request handler or a middleware that
runs on the worker pool of ANY chttpsvr instance.  Such a call is a
FATAL error: it calls ccol_fatal_err(), which is abort() and SIGABRT.
The library reports it at once, and does not hang.  The drain of the
worker pool of that server can never finish while this one in-flight
request itself blocks here and waits for the engine to exit.  That
deadlocks the shutdown of the whole shared engine, and not only of one
server.  An admin or shutdown endpoint that wants a full drain before
it answers calls chttpsvr_engine_stop() and then returns.  Call this
function from a separate thread.

After this function returns, the fallback engine logger is closed.  A
logger from chttpsvr_set_engine_logger stays in place, and the engine
that a later chttpsvr_start brings up logs through it again.

It is safe to call this function before the reactor ever starts, where
it does nothing.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_start (3),
.BR chttpsvr_set_engine_logger (3)
